<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibrahim Adil Shah II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1571">1571</a>–<a href="/years/1627">1627</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibrahim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah">Adil Shah</a> II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> from 1580 to 1627, under whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> sultanate reached its greatest extent and prosperity. A musician and poet, he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitab-i-nauras">Kitab-i-Nauras</a>, songs in Dakhni, and was tolerant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and different Muslim sects. His tomb complex, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-rauza">Ibrahim Rauza</a>, is one of the finest buildings of the Deccan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-ii.jpg" alt="Ibrahim Adil Shah II" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Ali Riza (The Bodleian Painter) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Ibrahim_Adil_Shah_II_(close_up),_dated_circa_1615.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
